Below is a minimal Java‑style snippet that shows how you would invoke the `NodeIdConverter.format` helper (exposed through the `functions.NodeIdConverter_format` function) to append the node‑ID to a `StringBuilder` called `logBuilder`.  
The function expects **string representations** of the arguments, so we simply pass the objects themselves (the runtime will convert them to strings).

```java
// Assume these objects already exist in your code:
LogEvent logEvent   = /* your LogEvent instance */;
StringBuilder logBuilder = new StringBuilder();

// Append the node‑ID (if available) to the StringBuilder
functions.NodeIdConverter_format({
    event: logEvent,          // the LogEvent that contains the node‑ID
    toAppendTo: logBuilder    // the StringBuilder to which the node‑ID will be appended
});

// After the call, logBuilder contains the original content plus the node‑ID
System.out.println(logBuilder.toString());
```

### What happens under the hood

* `NodeIdConverter.format` (the Java method behind the function) checks whether the `LogEvent` contains a node‑ID.  
* If it does, it appends that ID to the supplied `StringBuilder`.  
* If the node‑ID is not present, the `StringBuilder` remains unchanged.

### Quick sanity check

If you want to verify that the node‑ID was actually appended, you can add a simple check:

```java
String result = logBuilder.toString();
if (result.contains("nodeId=")) {
    System.out.println("Node ID successfully appended.");
} else {
    System.out.println("No node ID found in the LogEvent.");
}
```

That’s all you need—just call the helper with the `LogEvent` and the `StringBuilder`, and the node‑ID will be appended automatically when available.

